The documentation set is complete enough to begin the build. The recommended build order, given the dependency graph:

1. storage/db.js + storage/crypto.js + schemas + validator — get persistence and validation right first; everything else depends on it.
2. providers/base.js + groq.js + mock.js — the mock provider lets you test the entire app without any API key, including the orchestration loop.
3. orchestration/delegation.js + unit tests — the parser is the single riskiest piece of logic; test it against the mock provider before wiring real models.
4. orchestration/jennifer.js + prompt builders — wire the three-call turn end-to-end in the console before touching UI.
5. UI shell + chat + composer — the minimum viable surface to exercise the loop.
6. Settings, experts, guardrails, library, wizard — in that order, each behind its drawer.
7. Import/export, accessibility pass, mobile pass, manual test checklist.

The seed expert prompts in §4 above are the highest-leverage artifact to review carefully before code — they are what will make Hedetangen's output feel like Hedetangen and not like a generic glamping brand. If any of the six roles reads wrong for the business, adjust the prompt now, not after the build.